        "passage": "<b>THE RETURN OF THE EUROPEAN BEAVER</b>\nAcross the river valleys of Britain and continental Europe, a master engineer is quietly returning after centuries of absence. <b>[I]</b> <u><b>Hunted to near-extinction for its waterproof fur and scent glands by the nineteenth century, the Eurasian beaver is now staging a remarkable comeback that is reshaping flood-management policy.</b></u> Ecologists regard the rodent as a classic ‘keystone species’ whose dam-building habits create wetland habitats teeming with life.\nThe disappearance of beavers coincided with the industrial straightening of Europe’s waterways. Over the past two hundred years, engineers dredged riverbeds, cleared bankside willows and encased streams in concrete channels to drain farmland as rapidly as possible. <b>[II]</b> While this approach maximised arable acreage, it stripped catchments of their natural sponges, causing rainwater to race downstream and inundate towns during winter storms. Today, climate change is bringing more intense downpours, and conventional concrete flood walls are proving both <u><b>prohibitive</b></u> to maintain and vulnerable to overtopping.\nThe hydrological impact of beaver reintroduction is striking. In a five-year monitoring project on the River Otter in Devon, researchers documented how a single family of beavers constructed a sequence of thirteen leaky woody dams across a headwater stream. <b>[III]</b> During storm events, these structures slowed peak water flow by up to 30 percent and held back thousands of cubic metres of runoff in temporary ponds, releasing it gradually over the following days.\nDownstream communities soon noticed the difference. In the flood-prone village of East Budleigh, peak river levels during heavy autumn rains dropped markedly after beavers colonised the upper catchment, sparing dozens of cottages that had flooded repeatedly in the previous decade. <u><b>They</b></u> also acted as natural bio-filters: sediment, agricultural phosphates and nitrates settled out in the beaver ponds, leaving water downstream measurably cleaner.\n<b>[IV]</b> Ecological recovery followed swiftly. Within three years of the dams being built, surveys recorded a fivefold increase in native brown trout biomass, alongside the return of kingfishers, water shrews and twelve species of dragonflies. Amphibians such as common frogs spawned in the sunlit shallows created where beavers coppiced overhanging alders. In 2020, the UK government formally granted the River Otter beavers permanent legal status to remain in the wild, a decision that has begun to <u><b>gain traction</b></u> across other catchment authorities.\nCoexistence, nevertheless, requires active management. In flat agricultural fens, beaver burrows can undermine flood embankments, and dams in drainage ditches occasionally waterlog high-value crops. Conservation trusts in Bavaria and Scotland have responded by training ‘beaver officers’ who install simple plastic ‘beaver deceiver’ pipes through dams to regulate water height without destroying the lodge. Professor Richard Brazier, who led the Exeter University study, stresses that beavers are not a panacea for every urban flood. Even so, he concludes, restoring nature’s own hydro-engineers offers a cost-effective, self-maintaining ally in an era of climate extremes.\n<i>(Adapted from University of Exeter River Otter Beaver Trial Science and Evidence Report)</i>"
